            <ArticleTitle>Non-pharmacological lowering of low-density lipoprotein by apheresis and surgical techniques.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>575-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the past year, new data have appeared on the long-term benefits of low-density lipoprotein apheresis in severely hypercholesterolemic patients who are refractory to lipid-lowering drug therapy. Such data are critical for clinical decision-making, because they confirm the hypothesis that the dramatic reduction in low-density lipoprotein made possible by this technique produces clear-cut clinical benefits. Because of its efficacy and low incidence of side-effects, apheresis for severe drug-refractory hypercholesterolemia has superseded surgical approaches, such as liver transplantation or ileal bypass.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
